<div id="content">
  <h3 class="linklist">Bolex Reporter, Volume 6</h3>
  <!-- EPHEMERA and bolex reporter stuff -->
  <div id="books"> 
    <ul>
      <li><img class="fltright" src="../images/reporter/06_1.jpg" alt="Bolex Reporter" width="220" height="282"></li>
      <li class="bolrep">Christmas 1955</li>
      <li class="bolrep">Volume 6, Number 1</li>
      <li class="repart"><strong>Articles:</strong> "Make it a Bolex Movie Christmas", 
        Peggy Thompson; "Actors Screen Test with Bolex", Pat Sexton; "Better Movies 
        with Kern Paillard Lenses", Ernst Wildi; "Simple Animation Techniques: 
        Part Two", Peter Gibbons; "The New Pan Cinor 70 at the Summit", Robert 
        Hess; "Rock-a-bye Baby and Bolex", Mary Eleanor Browning; "Bolex Factory 
        Service", Charles Huettenmoser</li>
      <li class="desc">Robert Hess, a CBS news film photographer, discusses using 
        the new Pan Cinor 70 zoom lens while filming footage of the Big Four Summit 
        in Geneva.</li>
      <li class="pp">32 pages</li>
    </ul>
    <hr>
    <ul>
      <li><img class="fltleft" src="../images/reporter/06_2.jpg" alt="Bolex Reporter" width="220" height="282"></li>
      <li class="bolrep">Spring 1956</li>
      <li class="bolrep">Volume 6, Number 2</li>
      <li class="repart"><strong>Articles:</strong> "How to film your Best Girl 
        with Bolex", Ernst Wildi; "Titling Your Bolex Eight Millimeter Movies", 
        Peter Gibbons; "Bolex for Car Safety Research", A.J. White; "Movie Making 
        for 'Teen Agers", Peter Rauch; "Time Your Movies", Raymond Baran</li>
      <li class="desc">"Movie Making for 'Teen Agers" is a story about a group 
        of Bronx kids who make short films with their Bolex H-8. The author, Peter 
        Rauch, was one of the young members of the group. It's a really entertaining 
        article and makes me wonder whatever happened to them or the films they 
        made.</li>
      <li class="pp">32 pages</li>
    </ul>
    <hr>
    <ul>
      <li><img class="fltright" src="../images/reporter/06_3.jpg" alt="Bolex Reporter" width="220" height="281"></li>
      <li class="bolrep">Summer 1956</li>
      <li class="bolrep">Volume 6, Number 3</li>
      <li class="repart"><strong>Articles:</strong> "Bolex Stars in Hollywood", 
        Thomas H. Elwell; "The Naked Sea", Allen H. Miner; "How Bolex helped me 
        in Hollywood", Pat Sexton; "Gloria and Gene", Gene Lester; "Bolex Confidential", 
        Irvin Kersher; "Walt Disney, the Sixteen MM King", Joe Reddy</li>
      <li class="desc">Doris Day is featured on the cover of this Hollywood themed 
        issue of the Bolex Reporter. It focuses mainly on some of the current 
        Bolex users in Hollywood; stars such as Bob Hope, Bing Crosby, Gary Cooper, 
        Doris Day, Dorothy McGuire, Kim Novak and others. <a href="http://www.imdb.com/name/nm0786678/">Pat 
        Sexton</a> tells the story of how Bolex helped launch his career in Hollywood.</li>
      <li class="pp">32 pages</li>
    </ul>
    <hr>
    <ul>
      <li><img class="fltleft" src="../images/reporter/06_4.jpg" alt="Bolex Reporter" width="220" height="281"></li>
      <li class="bolrep">Fall 1956</li>
      <li class="bolrep">Volume 6, Number 4</li>
      <li class="repart"><strong>Articles:</strong> "Maintaining Bolex Supremacy", 
        Charles Huettenmoser; "How to Hollywood Edit your Bolex Movies", Peter 
        Gibbons; "Bolex Service", Charles Huettenmoser; "Marlinspike Movie Making", 
        Ray Jewell; "Doing the Impossible Immediately", Mark Steven</li>
      <li class="desc">Charles Huettenmoser explains the benefits of the patented 
        "Registrator Claw", or double claw registration in the Bolex H-16 Supreme 
        and later H models. Kim Novak appears on the cover, with her B-8 camera.</li>
      <li class="pp">32 pages</li>
    </ul>
